          ]
        },
        "grain": "customer",
        "as_of_constraint": null
      },
      {
        "name": "HighSpender",
        "formula": {
          "op": "expression",
          "expression": "MonthlyCharges > 70",
          "source_columns": [
            "MonthlyCharges"
          ]
        },
        "grain": "customer",
        "as_of_constraint": null
      },
      {
        "name": "NewCustomer",
        "formula": {
          "op": "expression",
          "expression": "tenure <= 6",
          "source_columns": [
            "tenure"
          ]
        },
        "grain": "customer",
        "as_of_constraint": null
      }
    ],
    "reasoning": "I selected a broad, tree-friendly feature set because this is a large dataset (~416k train rows) and the strongest signals in the analysis are spread across both numeric and categorical variables. I prioritized all raw features with meaningful mutual information, especially Contract (MI 0.1444), TotalCharges (0.1376), PaymentMethod (0.1207), OnlineSecurity (0.1145), InternetService (0.1109), TechSupport (0.1101), tenure (0.1054), and MonthlyCharges (0.0949). Group summaries also show large churn-rate separation for Contract, InternetService, Partner, Dependents, and related service variables, so they should be retained. No columns were flagged as leakage. Correlation analysis shows no near-duplicate pairs above the required |r| > 0.95 threshold, so both tenure and TotalCharges are kept despite moderate correlation (0.77), since trees can benefit from both and both have strong MI. I used ordinal encoding for most low-cardinality service/status categoricals because tree ensembles split efficiently on ordered integers and this avoids unnecessary feature explosion. I used one-hot for PaymentMethod and gender because the analysis suggested one-hot for these low-cardinality nominal fields; PaymentMethod has strong MI and meaningful category differences, while gender is weak (MI 0.0078) but still potentially useful in a large dataset. I also included the requested domain features that are likely to improve nonlinear separation: tenure buckets, average monthly spend proxy, charge ratio, tenure-spend interaction, service-count, no-internet flag, month-to-month flag, electronic-check flag, squared tenure, high-spender flag, and new-customer flag. These are domain-meaningful churn features rather than arbitrary transforms. I did not follow the diagnostic suggestion to log-transform SeniorCitizen because it is binary and tree-based models do not benefit from log scaling of a 0/1 indicator. All features are static at the customer row grain, so no temporal as-of constraint is needed. Note: the interaction feature tenure_x_ContractOrdinal is intended to capture tenure by contract strength; operationally, Contract should be ordinal-encoded first and then multiplied by tenure.",
    "excluded_columns": [
      "id: forbidden column and MI is negligible (0.0009), so it must not be used.",
      "Churn: target column, not a feature. Encode target Yes=1 and No=0 before training.",
      "No additional columns excluded for redundancy: no pair exceeded the near-duplicate threshold |correlation| > 0.95.",
      "No columns excluded for leakage: feature diagnostics flagged no high leakage risk.",
      "SeniorCitizen log1p transform excluded: suggested by diagnostics due to skew, but inappropriate because SeniorCitizen is already binary and tree models do not need this transform."
    ]
  },
